// ═══ WRONG: if — proceeds on false condition! ═══
synchronized (lock) {
    if (queue.isEmpty()) {     // CHECK once
        lock.wait();            // wait for notify
    }
    // DANGER: queue might STILL be empty!
    // Case 1: spurious wake-up (no notify called)
    // Case 2: another thread consumed the item after notify but before we reacquired lock
    String item = queue.poll(); // NULL! NullPointerException!
}

// ═══ CORRECT: while — rechecks after every wake-up ═══
synchronized (lock) {
    while (queue.isEmpty()) {  // CHECK every time we wake up!
        lock.wait();            // wait for notify (or spurious wake-up)
    }
    // If we're here: queue is DEFINITELY not empty!
    // We verified AFTER reacquiring the lock.
    String item = queue.poll(); // guaranteed non-null!
}

// ═══ SCENARIO: state stolen between notify and reacquire ═══
// Thread-A (consumer): synchronized(lock) { while(empty) lock.wait(); }
// Thread-B (producer): synchronized(lock) { queue.add(item); lock.notify(); }
// Thread-C (another consumer): synchronized(lock) { while(empty) lock.wait(); }
//
// Timeline:
// 1. Thread-A and Thread-C both waiting (queue empty)
// 2. Thread-B adds item → notify() → wakes Thread-A
// 3. Thread-A moves from wait-set to entry-set (needs lock)
// 4. BUT: Thread-C ALSO wakes up (notifyAll) or already in entry-set
// 5. Thread-C acquires lock FIRST → polls the item → queue empty again
// 6. Thread-A acquires lock → queue is EMPTY! 
//    With if: queue.poll() → null → NPE!
//    With while: rechecks isEmpty() → true → goes back to wait() → safe!

// ═══ SPURIOUS WAKE-UP — real phenomenon ═══
// The JVM spec (§17.8.1) explicitly states:
// "A thread may also wake up without being notified, interrupted,
//  or timing out, a so-called spurious wakeup."
// This is because the underlying OS implementation (pthread_cond_wait on Linux)
// may return spuriously due to signals, kernel scheduling, or other events.
// It's RARE but LEGAL — and your code MUST handle it.
